  "title": "Anthropic replaced Git after AI agents hit 30,000 merges a day",
  "narration": "Anthropic's AI agents wrote so much code, Git became the bottleneck. In May: twenty-five hundred merges a day. By September? More than thirty thousand. That's over twelve times the merge traffic. And the main branch history? Nearly a hundred gigabytes. CI alone ran about twenty-five million checkouts every day. That's an awful lot of repositories to drag around. So Anthropic built Scion, its own source-control system. It assumes agents are always online. Instead of cloning everything, clients ask servers for just the data they need. History, diffs, and even merges happen server-side. Seven engineers built Scion and its review tool in five months. But don't uninstall Git. Anthropic says Scion isn't meant for ordinary repositories. For most teams, Git still makes sense. Thirty thousand merges a day changes the rules.",
